1
00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:04.471
Having learned how to parse and extract information from your files,

2
00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:07.807
you'll now learn how to answer questions about these files.

3
00:00:08.375 --> 00:00:12.679
In this lesson, you'll build a RAG application to query your PDFs.

4
00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:15.782
You'll first use ADE to parse the documents.

5
00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:18.885
Then store the parsed chunks in a vector database

6
00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:22.088
and retrieve them to answer questions about your documents.

7
00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:25.158
Let's dive in.

8
00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:28.161
Up to now, we've been learning about document AI,

9
00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:31.164
why OCR fails, how to best utilize layout,

10
00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:33.066
and why reading order matters.

11
00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:36.603
and how LandingAI's agentic document extraction gives us clean,

12
00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:39.606
grounded output by combining all the complexities

13
00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:42.108
of document processing in a single

14
00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:44.110
unified workflow.

15
00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:46.613
So now the obvious question is,

16
00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:48.615
what do we do with all this structured data?

17
00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:51.618
One possible answer is building real systems.

18
00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:54.120
Specifically, we'll build a

19
00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.323
Retrieval-Augmented Generation pipeline or a RAG system

20
00:00:57.624 --> 00:01:01.694
that turns a 74-page financial filing into a

21
00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:04.497
living breathing document and a queryable knowledge base.

22
00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:09.502
This is the same architectural pattern powering modern document QA systems

23
00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:12.906
in production today across all industries and verticals.

24
00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:15.208
Let's frame this in a real scenario.

25
00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:18.511
Imagine you're building an internal platform for a hedge fund.

26
00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:22.515
Your analysts have SEC filings like this Apple 10-K.

27
00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:25.919
It contains detailed financial and business information

28
00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:28.221
that publicly traded companies must disclose

29
00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:30.323
to the security and exchange commission.

30
00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:34.094
The analysts want to upload the files to the platform

31
00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:37.797
and ask questions like, what was Apple's net sales in 2023?

32
00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:40.033
What are the biggest business risks?

33
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.302
How did the services revenue trend year-over-year?

34
00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:45.505
The information they need is in the document, somewhere.

35
00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:48.208
Maybe in a table on page 28,

36
00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:50.710
maybe in a footnote on page 45,

37
00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.446
or spread across three different risk

38
00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:56.316
disclosures on pages 12, 15, and 18.

39
00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:58.718
Now, why does traditional keyword

40
00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:02.088
search fail here? First is Semantic mismatch.

41
00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:04.691
If an analyst searches for revenue,

42
00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:07.494
but if the document uses phrases like net sales,

43
00:02:07.494 --> 00:02:09.896
traditional search would return nothing.

44
00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:13.700
The concepts are identical, but the words don't match exactly.

45
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:16.102
Second is Context blindness.

46
00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:19.906
The word revenue may appear 75 times in this document.

47
00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:22.008
And if you want to know which

48
00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:24.077
mention is relevant to the analyst's question,

49
00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:27.280
keyword search can't tell without additional guidance.

50
00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.582
And this isn't scalable.

51
00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:32.285
Third is fragmentation. Information is scattered.

52
00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:35.288
To fully answer the question, what are the risks,

53
00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:38.291
you need to synthesize content from multiple pages.

54
00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:41.294
Traditional search can't do that. You need semantic understanding.

55
00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:43.496
You also need context-aware retrieval,

56
00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:45.231
which means you need a system

57
00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:47.267
that understands what the user is asking,

58
00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:50.670
not just matches strings. This is where RAG comes in,

59
00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.273
which is what you will build in the lab for this lesson.

60
00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:56.075
So what is RAG and why does it matter?

61
00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:59.579
RAG, or retrieval-augmented generation, is the architecture behind

62
00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:03.283
nearly every modern document Q&amp;A system today.

63
00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:06.286
The pipeline has six steps across three core phases.

64
00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:08.288
First is preprocessing phase.

65
00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:10.590
You parse, embed, then store.

66
00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:15.562
Parsing takes raw documents and parse and extract clean, structured text.

67
00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:18.331
This is where ADE comes in. Clean

68
00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:20.767
input is critical. Garbage in, garbage out.

69
00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:24.571
If your parsing is noisy with OCR errors and mangled tables

70
00:03:24.571 --> 00:03:27.574
that you saw in previous lessons, everything downstream suffers.

71
00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:30.577
Embedding converts parsed content

72
00:03:30.577 --> 00:03:33.580
into embeddings or vectors that capture semantic meaning.

73
00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.549
Storing saves these vectors

74
00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:39.252
in a vector database optimized for similarity search.

75
00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:42.755
You will use ChromaDB, a local, open-source option

76
00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:45.258
perfect for development and learning.

77
00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:48.962
Second is the retrieval phase. Query and retrieve.

78
00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:52.465
Querying embeds the user's question, searches through the database

79
00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:56.669
to find the top-ranking vectors as measured by similarity,

80
00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:59.739
in other words, the most similar vectors to the query.

81
00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:03.943
Retrieving filters out any vectors whose similarity might be too low,

82
00:04:03.943 --> 00:04:06.946
then fetches the rest. Third is the generation phase.

83
00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:09.949
It feeds the retrieve content as context

84
00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:12.151
to a language model to generate

85
00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:15.154
a natural language answer with the corresponding

86
00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:18.424
retrieve content for grounding and verification.

87
00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:21.327
This is critical for heavily regulated organizations

88
00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:23.830
or HROs like financial services,

89
00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.132
healthcare, and life sciences.

90
00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:29.235
Now here's a key insight that ties back everything you've

91
00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:32.038
ADE's clean grounded output

92
00:04:32.038 --> 00:04:35.308
and phase one is what makes this entire pipeline possible.

93
00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:37.510
If your parsing is unreliable

94
00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:40.813
and if you're feeding the system distorted OCR outputs

95
00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:45.118
or missing tables, charts, and graphs with visual storytelling,

96
00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:48.521
no amount of clever embeddings, prompt engineering,

97
00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:50.823
or sophisticated retrieval will save you.

98
00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:54.994
By the end of this lesson's lab, you will understand how ADE

99
00:04:54.994 --> 00:04:57.897
output flows directly into a RAG system,

100
00:04:58.331 --> 00:05:01.934
build a local RAG pipeline using ChromaDB

101
00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:04.804
and embeddings generated by an OpenAI model,

102
00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:08.875
run semantic search, not keyword search, over real documents,

103
00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:12.812
verify every result visually using grounding images.

104
00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:17.116
Then be fully prepared to scale and deploy this system to AWS.

105
00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:19.352
We'll cover this in lesson six. So you

106
00:05:19.352 --> 00:05:21.387
may be wondering, why are we building locally?

107
00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:24.824
Why not jump straight to AWS for a real production system?

108
00:05:25.024 --> 00:05:27.193
That's a great question. And there are three reasons.

109
00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:29.429
number one is faster iteration.

110
00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:33.866
You can change code, rerun a cell, see results in two seconds

111
00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:36.502
with no deployment overhead, no debugging.

112
00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:38.504
When you're learning, speed matters.

113
00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:40.540
Reason number two is lower cost.

114
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.142
Experimenting locally is essentially free,

115
00:05:43.543 --> 00:05:45.645
after the initial API calls.

116
00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:48.648
You're not burning cloud compute credits while you learn and debug.

117
00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:50.850
Reason three is clearer learning.

118
00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:54.354
You can strip away the cloud complexity to focus

119
00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:56.956
purely on the RAG mechanics and data flow.

120
00:05:57.457 --> 00:06:00.360
In lesson six, we'll take this exact logic

121
00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.961
and productionize it on AWS.

122
00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:04.330
Same data flow at cloud scale.

123
00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.833
But you'll build the foundation in this lab.

124
00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:09.635
In particular, here's what you'll use in the lab.

125
00:06:09.769 --> 00:06:12.972
The input will be Apple's 10-K from the previous fiscal year,

126
00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:16.275
a 74-page PDF of dense financial reporting,

127
00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:19.278
which is a real document with real-world complexity.

128
00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:22.248
The parser, ADE API from LandingAI.

129
00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:24.550
The outputs are provided to you,

130
00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:26.753
so you won't be doing the parsing step.

131
00:06:26.953 --> 00:06:29.956
This process was well covered in the previous lesson with Andrea.

132
00:06:30.256 --> 00:06:34.560
The output, markdown text plus JSON chunks with metadata.

133
00:06:34.761 --> 00:06:38.164
Clean, structured data ready for embedding.

134
00:06:38.164 --> 00:06:42.468
Embeddings will be generated using OpenAI's Text Embedding 3 Small model.

135
00:06:42.769 --> 00:06:45.471
We're using their API for convenience,

136
00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:48.775
but you could swap it out for any open source alternatives.

137
00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:51.978
Vector database, ChromaDB running locally

138
00:06:51.978 --> 00:06:54.781
in the lab environment with persistent storage.

139
00:06:54.981 --> 00:06:56.916
Let's start with the first step

140
00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:59.185
of the pre-processing phase, parsing with ADE.

141
00:06:59.786 --> 00:07:02.188
When you parse a document with ADE,

142
00:07:02.188 --> 00:07:04.690
You will get back a ParseResponse object.

143
00:07:05.091 --> 00:07:06.692
Let's break down its structure.

144
00:07:06.893 --> 00:07:10.997
At the top, you have parse_result.splits, which is a list.

145
00:07:11.197 --> 00:07:15.701
One split per page when you pass the split equals page parameter.

146
00:07:16.002 --> 00:07:18.404
Each split has two key attributes.

147
00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:22.008
First is the .markdown. This is the clean

148
00:07:22.041 --> 00:07:24.544
markdown text extracted from that page.

149
00:07:24.844 --> 00:07:27.814
Tables become markdown tables with pipes and dashes.

150
00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:30.817
Headings become markdown headings with hash symbols.

151
00:07:31.217 --> 00:07:33.519
It's human readable, structured text,

152
00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:36.522
not a wall of unformatted OCR mess.

153
00:07:36.823 --> 00:07:39.826
It's also LLM ready for downstream use cases.

154
00:07:40.226 --> 00:07:41.828
Second is .chunks

155
00:07:41.861 --> 00:07:43.863
This is a list of chunk IDs.

156
00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:46.666
Each chunk is a piece of content on the page.

157
00:07:47.066 --> 00:07:50.770
A paragraph, a table, a figure, or a caption.

158
00:07:51.070 --> 00:07:53.372
For each chunk, you also have metadata.

159
00:07:53.773 --> 00:07:58.344
chunk_id, a UUID that uniquely identifies this piece of content.

160
00:07:58.744 --> 00:08:01.047
The text, the extracted content itself.

161
00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:04.050
chunk_type, which tells you what kind of content this is.

162
00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:09.450
text, tables, figures, attestation, logo, etc. bbox.

163
00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:13.893
Bounding box coordinates showing exactly where on the page this chunk appeared.

164
00:08:14.293 --> 00:08:16.796
page. Page number where the chunk appears.

165
00:08:17.096 --> 00:08:20.900
And importantly, for every chunk, you can generate a grounding image,

166
00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.302
a visual crop from the original PDF.

167
00:08:23.703 --> 00:08:27.507
This gives you the ability to debug when you're building your pipeline,

168
00:08:27.507 --> 00:08:31.277
and you can visually verify that extraction worked correctly. No guessing.

169
00:08:31.477 --> 00:08:34.380
You can build trust when your system tells a user

170
00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.083
net sales were 383 billion dollars.

171
00:08:37.116 --> 00:08:40.219
You can show them the exact table the information came from.

172
00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.121
They can see with their own eyes,

173
00:08:42.121 --> 00:08:44.624
which builds massive confidence in your system.

174
00:08:44.924 --> 00:08:48.928
Compliance. In regulated industries like finance, healthcare, and legal,

175
00:08:49.128 --> 00:08:51.130
you need to prove where information came from.

176
00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.934
Grounding images give you that audit trail. No hallucinations.

177
00:08:55.334 --> 00:08:58.938
Since you've already seen how to call the parse API with Andrea,

178
00:08:59.038 --> 00:09:00.940
we'll skip this step in the lab.

179
00:09:01.107 --> 00:09:04.310
You'll be directly provided with these ADE outputs.

180
00:09:04.744 --> 00:09:07.246
The next step in preprocessing

181
00:09:07.246 --> 00:09:09.549
is to embed the text of each ADE's chunk.

182
00:09:09.549 --> 00:09:11.450
You will transform each chunk's text

183
00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:13.653
into an embedding vector of size 1536.

184
00:09:15.254 --> 00:09:19.759
Each embedding vector will mathematically encode the meaning of the parsed text

185
00:09:19.759 --> 00:09:23.663
so that semantically similar text gets a similar vector.

186
00:09:24.063 --> 00:09:25.464
So when you ask a question about

187
00:09:25.464 --> 00:09:26.866
your document, you can retrieve the chunks

188
00:09:26.866 --> 00:09:30.970
that have embeddings similar to the embeddings of your question.

189
00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.739
This is called chunk level embedding.

190
00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:36.208
The other option is to do Page-Level embedding,

191
00:09:36.208 --> 00:09:38.911
meaning you embed the entire text of a page.

192
00:09:38.911 --> 00:09:40.613
Let's understand the tradeoffs.

193
00:09:40.613 --> 00:09:43.916
In practice, Page-Level is simple to implement,

194
00:09:43.916 --> 00:09:47.219
fewer embeddings, faster database build,

195
00:09:47.219 --> 00:09:50.623
and it can be effective for broad questions and shorter documents.

196
00:09:50.623 --> 00:09:53.626
Chunk-Level provides more precise retrieval,

197
00:09:53.626 --> 00:09:56.228
exact table, paragraph matching,

198
00:09:56.228 --> 00:09:59.932
more granular context, and is better for focused questions.

199
00:10:00.099 --> 00:10:02.101
with complex documents.

200
00:10:02.101 --> 00:10:04.904
And with ADE, we can even give you cell level groundings

201
00:10:04.904 --> 00:10:06.706
for complex tables.

202
00:10:07.306 --> 00:10:09.108
In the lab, you'll use chunk level approach,

203
00:10:09.108 --> 00:10:12.478
but in production, you can tune this based on your use case.

204
00:10:12.878 --> 00:10:14.880
The last step in preprocessing

205
00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.283
is to store the vectors in a vector database.

206
00:10:17.283 --> 00:10:21.087
We're using ChromaDB because it's ideal for learning and prototyping.

207
00:10:21.487 --> 00:10:23.289
You have one-line install,

208
00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:27.760
persistent storage, since ChromaDB saves your vectors into your disk.

209
00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:30.162
automatically. If you close your notebook and come

210
00:10:30.162 --> 00:10:32.565
back tomorrow, your data will still be there.

211
00:10:32.665 --> 00:10:36.569
You don't have to re-embed everything. This is crucial for efficient iteration.

212
00:10:36.969 --> 00:10:41.969
Fast Similarity Search. ChromaDB uses HNSW indexing under the hood.

213
00:10:42.274 --> 00:10:44.944
Hierarchical Navigable Small World graphs.

214
00:10:45.044 --> 00:10:49.248
This is a state-of-the-art algorithm for approximate nearest neighbor search.

215
00:10:49.548 --> 00:10:53.552
Queries return in milliseconds, even with thousands of vectors.

216
00:10:53.753 --> 00:10:55.421
You'll have same API locally

217
00:10:55.421 --> 00:10:59.225
and in production. ChromaDB has a client server mode.

218
00:10:59.225 --> 00:11:02.328
The same API you use locally works in production

219
00:11:02.328 --> 00:11:04.330
with a remote ChromaDB server.

220
00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:07.633
This is huge. No mental model shift when you scale.

221
00:11:07.633 --> 00:11:10.336
And finally, rich metadata support.

222
00:11:10.336 --> 00:11:14.240
For each chunk, you can store metadata that can help with filtering.

223
00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:16.942
allowing you to retrieve items

224
00:11:16.942 --> 00:11:20.146
based on specific criteria within their metadata.

225
00:11:20.613 --> 00:11:23.082
In the lab, you'll store the chunk_type, page

226
00:11:23.082 --> 00:11:26.619
number, and the coordinates of the bounding boxes

227
00:11:26.619 --> 00:11:28.187
as metadata for each chunk.

228
00:11:28.187 --> 00:11:29.755
When you add a chunk to

229
00:11:29.755 --> 00:11:31.590
ChromaDB, you need to specify an ID.

230
00:11:31.791 --> 00:11:35.294
You use the exact chunk ID that is provided from ADE.

231
00:11:35.795 --> 00:11:38.898
Note that in lesson six, we'll swap this for AWS Bedrock

232
00:11:38.898 --> 00:11:41.901
Knowledge Base, but the concept stays identical.

233
00:11:42.101 --> 00:11:44.103
After you set up the vector database,

234
00:11:44.103 --> 00:11:47.306
you'll write a function that you'll use in the retrieval step.

235
00:11:47.506 --> 00:11:50.609
Let's walk through exactly what happens when you run it.

236
00:11:50.776 --> 00:11:54.113
for what was Apple's net sales in 2023?

237
00:11:54.513 --> 00:11:56.882
First, we'll embed the question and it

238
00:11:56.882 --> 00:11:59.251
will be transformed into an embedding vector.

239
00:11:59.552 --> 00:12:04.256
Same embedding model used during preprocessing and same size for the vector.

240
00:12:04.557 --> 00:12:09.361
Second, search. You'll pass that query vector to ChromaDB and say,

241
00:12:09.462 --> 00:12:11.363
find me the top k chunks

242
00:12:11.363 --> 00:12:13.566
that are closest to this query vector.

243
00:12:13.966 --> 00:12:15.801
By default, k equals 3. So

244
00:12:15.801 --> 00:12:17.970
we want the three most similar chunks.

245
00:12:18.437 --> 00:12:21.440
You can adjust this based on your use case. Third is score.

246
00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:23.642
ChromaDB also returns distance metrics.

247
00:12:23.743 --> 00:12:26.045
You convert the distance metrics to similarity.

248
00:12:26.345 --> 00:12:28.447
Similarity equals 1 minus distance.

249
00:12:28.748 --> 00:12:30.549
Higher similarity means better match.

250
00:12:30.649 --> 00:12:32.351
This is a parameter you can

251
00:12:32.351 --> 00:12:34.320
adjust to further fine-tune your RAG engine.

252
00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:39.024
Fourth is filter. You'll then remove results based on similarity thresholds.

253
00:12:39.125 --> 00:12:43.028
Then you can visualize. Finally, you'll display the chunk text,

254
00:12:43.129 --> 00:12:45.731
IDs, scores, page numbers.

255
00:12:46.098 --> 00:12:48.200
and types for the return results.

256
00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:50.302
You will also use the coordinates of

257
00:12:50.302 --> 00:12:52.605
the bounding boxes to display the grounding images.

258
00:12:52.705 --> 00:12:55.107
This is one of my favorite features of ADE,

259
00:12:55.107 --> 00:12:59.912
and is something that differentiates LandingAI from commodity document AI systems.

260
00:13:00.012 --> 00:13:04.617
For every chunk that ADE extracts, you can generate a grounding image.

261
00:13:04.717 --> 00:13:06.352
This is a PNG file showing

262
00:13:06.352 --> 00:13:08.287
the visual crop of that specific chunk

263
00:13:08.287 --> 00:13:10.289
from the original PDF.

264
00:13:10.589 --> 00:13:13.292
Let me tell you why this is so crucial in practice.

265
00:13:13.759 --> 00:13:18.264
This builds massive trust. Users aren't just blindly accepting answers

266
00:13:18.264 --> 00:13:20.966
or getting fooled by LLM hallucinations.

267
00:13:20.966 --> 00:13:23.135
They're verifying them. And when they verify

268
00:13:23.135 --> 00:13:25.271
a few answers and see they're correct,

269
00:13:25.271 --> 00:13:27.973
they trust the system for future queries.

270
00:13:27.973 --> 00:13:29.575
This will ensure adoption.

271
00:13:29.775 --> 00:13:32.278
Grounding images gives you that paper trail

272
00:13:32.278 --> 00:13:36.282
and opportunities to introduce human in the loop for risk mitigation.

273
00:13:36.582 --> 00:13:39.285
Imagine a financial analyst using your system

274
00:13:39.285 --> 00:13:41.453
to pull data for a quarterly report.

275
00:13:41.453 --> 00:13:43.722
Six months later, if the auditors ask

276
00:13:43.722 --> 00:13:45.624
where did this number come from,

277
00:13:45.624 --> 00:13:48.027
they can say it came from page 28,

278
00:13:48.027 --> 00:13:50.629
table 3, row 5, column 6

279
00:13:50.629 --> 00:13:52.731
of the Q3 10-K filing.

280
00:13:52.731 --> 00:13:55.734
And here's the visual proof, which is really powerful.

281
00:13:56.235 --> 00:13:59.738
You will finally incorporate what you've learned into a full RAG pipeline.

282
00:14:00.239 --> 00:14:03.342
And for that, you'll be using LangChain for orchestration.

283
00:14:03.943 --> 00:14:06.545
taking advantage of a pre-built chain

284
00:14:06.545 --> 00:14:10.616
that combines the retrieval and generation phases of a RAG pipeline.

285
00:14:10.616 --> 00:14:14.620
You will create a retrieval object from the ChromaDB database.

286
00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:17.623
Retrievers are components of LangChain that fetches information

287
00:14:17.623 --> 00:14:22.294
and slots into prompt as added context.

288
00:14:22.895 --> 00:14:25.898
Sometimes a retriever might provide too many chunks.

289
00:14:25.898 --> 00:14:29.101
Given a limited context window of LLMs,

290
00:14:29.101 --> 00:14:32.905
LangChain can combine multiple chunks into a single prompt

291
00:14:32.905 --> 00:14:36.609
or sequence many chunks over several prompts iteratively.

292
00:14:36.909 --> 00:14:40.579
All right, let's build a real RAG system with ADE.
